Title: AutoCAD orphaned when alfe is killed or the job is cancelled (runner-side sweep)
Status: DONE (alfe 2.2.87, 2026-09-25; deferred 2026-09-16, taken up on pjb's go-ahead)
Split from: issues/open/alfe-autocad-automation-never-quits-acad.issue
            (its FIX part B; part A shipped in alfe 2.2.71)

WHAT REMAINS

alfe 2.2.71 makes a run quit the AutoCAD it CREATED, at shutdown. That
closes the path that was leaving one behind every time -- including
after successful runs -- but it can only act when shutdown is REACHED.

Two endings never reach it:

  - alfe is killed (the runner kills the job's process on cancel);
  - the job is cancelled or times out while alfe is mid-run.

And nothing the job can do reaches the process anyway. In automation
mode alfe runs CSCRIPT, which asks Windows' COM service for an
AutoCAD.Application; the acad.exe that answers is a child of THAT
SERVICE. It is not in the job's process tree, so even a tree kill from
the runner would not touch it. Only something that goes looking for it
by name can.

WHY IT IS DEFERRED AND NOT DROPPED

  - INCIDENCE IS LOW. Over 2026-09-05..09-15, 1,758 jobs on PF5S26B:
    exactly ONE GUI-mode AutoCAD job was cancelled mid-script
    (16463865287, 09-12), and NO job ended in job_execution_timeout.
    The one 1800 s AutoCAD job hung in `git fetch` before any CAD ran.
    So this is the rare path; the common one is fixed.
  - THE SCOPE IS A JUDGEMENT ABOUT SOMEONE ELSE'S MACHINE. The Windows
    runner is pjb's laptop, which he also uses. A sweep is the first
    thing here that would kill a process alfe did not start, and
    getting its scope wrong closes his AutoCAD. That is worth deciding
    deliberately rather than shipping alongside a bug fix.

WHAT IT WOULD LOOK LIKE, WHEN TAKEN UP

The distinguishing mark is the command line: an AutoCAD started by COM
carries /Automation; one a person launched does not.

    Get-CimInstance Win32_Process -Filter "Name='acad.exe'" |
      Where-Object CommandLine -match '/Automation'

Open questions, all of them decisions rather than facts:

  - BEFORE or AFTER each job, or both? Before is safer (it cleans up
    after the PREVIOUS job's bad ending, and cannot kill an AutoCAD the
    current job is using). After is tidier but must not race the
    session that just ended.
  - Which jobs carry it -- only the four automation ones
    (vendor:probes:autocad, path-sysvars / getcname / pgp:probe:
    autocad:windows), or every Windows CAD job?
  - Is /Automation enough of a guarantee on that laptop? If pjb ever
    drives AutoCAD from a script of his own, it would carry the same
    mark.
  - An `after_script` does NOT run when a job is cancelled hard, which
    is precisely the case this exists for -- so a before-job sweep is
    probably the only one that covers the motivating case.

HOW TO TELL WHETHER THIS STILL MATTERS

After 2.2.71 is on the runner, an orphaned AutoCAD means ONE of two
things, and they are distinguishable:

  - the preceding job ended in cancel/kill  -> this ticket's path, and
    evidence that the sweep is worth building;
  - the preceding job ended NORMALLY        -> 2.2.71 did not do what
    it claims, and THAT is the bug to chase, not this one.

Check the last job on PF5S26B before the orphan appeared. Do not assume
the first; the whole of the parent ticket was spent on mistaking one
explanation for another.

ALSO WORTH KNOWING: 2.2.71 quits only an instance alfe CREATED. While a
stale AutoCAD is still running on the laptop, every new run ATTACHES to
it and so will never quit it. The existing leftover has to be closed by
hand ONCE; after that each job creates and quits its own.

RESOLUTION -- alfe 2.2.87 (2026-09-25)

Taken up on pjb's go-ahead. The open questions above were decisions
about someone else's machine; here is what each was decided to be, and
why.

WHAT IS SWEPT: ONLY WHAT ALFE WROTE DOWN

Not "an acad.exe that looks like CI's". The bridge now records every
instance it CREATES -- PID and creation time, via WMI, since the
acad.exe COM starts is a child of the COM service and is in no process
tree alfe could walk -- into <temp>/alfe-created-cad.txt. The sweep ends
those, and only while BOTH the PID and the creation time still match.

That answers the question the issue was deferred over. "/Automation in
the command line" was the obvious mark, and it is not good enough: pjb
driving AutoCAD from a script of his own would carry the same mark on
the same laptop. A recorded identity cannot be mistaken; a resemblance
can. The creation time is not decoration either -- Windows reuses PIDs,
and a stale entry naming a recycled PID would otherwise name whatever
now holds it.

The file lives outside the workdir on purpose: the workdir dies with the
run, and the run this serves is the one that cleaned nothing up.

WHEN: BEFORE EACH CAD JOB

before_script on .gate-windows-cad-{hard,soft}, so every Windows CAD job
gets it, including ones added later. A hard cancel never runs an
after_script -- which is precisely the ending this exists for -- so
after-job could not cover the motivating case. Before is also the safe
moment: nothing the current job started can be caught by it.

The sweep reports what it finds and exits 0 whatever happens: a cleanup
must not fail the job it precedes.

WHICH JOBS: ALL THE CAD-TAGGED ONES

Not only the four automation probes. The `cad' tag is what routes a job
to the CAD-capable runner, so it is exactly the set that can inherit an
orphan, and the set that will keep matching as jobs are added.

TESTS (alfe SBCL and CCL, 1096 checks, 0 failures)

- the bridge template records PID and creation time, through WMI, and
  does so only on the CREATE path -- an ATTACHED instance is somebody
  else's and must never be swept (seen red: removing the call fails the
  test);
- the registry path is in the temp directory, not the workdir;
- EMIT-BRIDGE-VBS substitutes it, and an explicit NIL empties the
  placeholder rather than leaving it unexpanded.

The sweep script itself is PowerShell and is not exercised by the Lisp
suite; it runs on the Windows runner from the next CAD job onwards.

NOT DONE HERE

Killing a BricsCAD left behind the same way. BricsCAD's automation path
differs per platform (cscript on Windows, AppleScript on macOS) and no
orphan has been observed from it; the registry is named for CAD rather
than AutoCAD so the same mechanism can carry it when one is.
